body:
### Tested versions

4.3.stable

### System information

linux

### Issue description

The expected behaviour from the code is that a single thread (id) will for every print output true, and all others threads output false;

What happens is that:
In the first variation (tdr1) one gets a single true output, and for every subsequent all return false;
![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/78cf05b7-d675-4f7b-8e94-43bdddcaacb3)

in the second variantion (tdr2) every thread always print true.
![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c81850ea-9f3c-4506-9034-9395147cb419)




Both behaviours do not represent the expected one.

(i know the threads are floation on memory and there is no clean-up, this is just a example, but the behaviour persist with correct administration  of thread reference, but... Maybe this has something with the _odd_ patern "while true".)

--------
if you coment the await, you get a torrent of print-outs, but the behaviour is correct, every print of a single id is true, all others are false
![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/5016aca9-a047-413b-938a-251819003c98)


### Steps to reproduce


Uncoment the variant to run, or run both.
run this code on Node:

```
extends Node
func _ready() -> void:
	for each in 10:
		Thread.new().start(Callable(self,"tdr1"))
		#Thread.new().start(Callable(self,"tdr2"))

var mutex1 = Mutex.new()
func tdr1():
	var id = randi()
	while true:
		printt("tdr1",id,mutex1.try_lock())
		await get_tree().create_timer(60/60.0).timeout


var mutex2 = Mutex.new()
func tdr2():
	var id = randi()
	while true:
		await get_tree().create_timer(60/60.0).timeout
		printt("tdr2",id,mutex2.try_lock())

```

